module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.AffineChartDiagramCover
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ChartSliceFlatDescent
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.LocallyPrincipalPullback
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.Topologies.GlobalFunctionsAtlas

/-!
# Pulling the zero locus of global functions back along an arbitrary morphism

Let `g : X ⟶ Y` be a morphism of algebraic spaces over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf
site, let `f₁, …, f_r ∈ Γ(Y, 𝒪_Y)` and let `V(f₁, …, f_r) ⊆ Y` be the closed subspace they cut
out, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.vanishingSubfunctor`.  This file pulls that datum back along `g`
with **no hypothesis on `g`** — in particular with `g` smooth, which is the case a slicing
argument over an algebraic stack needs and which the étale restriction of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/VanishingSubspaceOpenRestriction.lean` does not cover.

Two things have to be pulled back: the functions, and the identification of the zero locus.

The functions need no new construction.  A global regular function on an algebraic space is the
same thing as a compatible family of regular functions on *all* of its scheme charts, not just
the étale ones — that is the atlas step
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.globalFunctionsChartEquiv` — and on all-chart families restriction is
precomposition, so `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.globalFunctionsMap` already restricts a global
regular function along an arbitrary morphism of algebraic spaces.  The two spellings of global
functions involved, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.globalFunctions'` and the
`(SheafOfModules.unit (structureRingSheaf B S hS X)).sections` that
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.vanishingIdealAt` consumes, are the *same type*, so no transport and no
second map is needed; what this file adds is the one computation of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.globalFunctionsMap` that the comparison of ideals requires.

The identification of the zero locus is pulled back *chartwise*.  On an affine étale chart `V`
of `X` which maps over `g` to an affine étale chart `U` of `Y`, the defining ideal of
`g⁻¹ V(f₁, …, f_r)` is the ideal generated by the restrictions `g^*f₁|_V, …, g^*f_r|_V`:
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_eq_map` says the defining ideal of an inverse
image on such a chart is the ideal generated by the image of the defining ideal upstairs, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_vanishingSubspace_eq'` says the latter is the ideal of
the family.  Nothing about `g` is used in either.

That chartwise identification is then globalised to *every* affine étale chart of `X`, which is
what the two bounds defining `V(f₁, …, f_r)` quantify over, and the conclusion is the equality
of closed subspaces `V(g^*f₁, …, g^*f_r) = g⁻¹ V(f₁, …, f_r)` — again with no hypothesis on
`g`, in particular for `g` smooth.  See the implementation notes for the three steps, and the
implementation notes of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Stacks/ChartSlicingRegularSequence.lean` for what consumes this
equality.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.globalFunctionsMap_val_of_factor`: the value of a pulled-back
  function on an étale chart `V` of `X` which maps over `g` to an étale chart `U` of `Y` is the
  pullback of the value on `U` along that morphism of schemes.  This is the only computation of
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.globalFunctionsMap` the file needs, and it is what makes the chartwise
  comparison of ideals possible.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.globalFunctionsMap_eq_unitSectionsRestrict`: on an **étale**
  morphism the restriction of global functions is the étale restriction
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.unitSectionsRestrict`.  So the étale file constructs no map the
  general one does not, and the general one is not a second copy of it.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_vanishingSubspace_eq_of_factor`: **the
  chartwise base change of the zero locus along an arbitrary morphism**, with no hypothesis.  On
  affine charts related by a map over `g`, the defining ideal of `g⁻¹ V(f₁, …, f_r)` is the ideal
  of the pulled-back family.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_vanishingSubspace_eq`: the same at **every**
  affine étale chart of `X`, with no hypothesis — the globalisation of the previous statement.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.vanishingSubfunctor_globalFunctionsMap_eq_preimage`: **the base
  change of `V(f₁, …, f_r)` along an arbitrary morphism of algebraic spaces**, with no
  hypothesis: `V(g^*f₁, …, g^*f_r) = g⁻¹ V(f₁, …, f_r)`.

## Implementation notes

`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.unitSectionsRestrict` cannot be generalised in place.  Its body reindexes
the étale charts of the source through
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.smallEtalePostcomp`, and that functor exists *only* for an étale
morphism: it is `(IsEtaleChart B S hS Y.1).lift` of postcomposition, and the hypothesis
`hf : IsEtale B S hS f.hom` is consumed exactly to show that an étale chart of `X` postcomposed
with `f` is still an *étale* chart of `Y`, which for a smooth `f` is false.  So the étale
hypothesis there is essential — to the construction.  It is not essential to the notion, and
the all-chart family of `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartFunctions` is the index category on which
the pullback exists in general.  Because the two agree where both are defined
(`globalFunctionsMap_eq_unitSectionsRestrict`), the étale statements of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/VanishingSubspaceOpenRestriction.lean` need no change and
keep their `rfl` proofs.

The comparison of ideals is proved first chartwise and then globalised, because
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.vanishingSubfunctor_le` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.le_vanishingSubfunctor` quantify over *every* affine étale chart of
`X` while the chartwise statement is available only at those which map over `g` to an affine
chart of `Y`.  The three steps of the globalisation all rest on statements the library already
has:

1. the charts of `X` which do so factor are **cover-dense**, so the arrows out of them generate
   a covering sieve of any chart.  This is the geometric step, and it is
   `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_topology_of_hasAffineChartDiagram` of
   `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/AffineChartDiagramCover.lean`, whose
   `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HasAffineChartDiagram` is exactly the datum the chartwise statement
   consumes; its proof base changes the atlas of `Y` and then makes the two charts affine in
   the order that survives the base change.
2. membership in the ideal of the family descends along that sieve.  The sheaf of ideals
   generated by finitely many global functions has the ideal of the family as its sections over
   an *affine* chart, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.vanishingIdealSheaf_obj_of_isAffine`, which is
   the étale descent of ideal membership behind
   `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_vanishingIdealSheaf`; being a submodule of a
   sheaf, its membership is local.
3. membership in the defining ideal of the inverse image descends along it as well,
   `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_definingIdealAt_of_sieve`, by separatedness of the direct image
   of the structure sheaf of the subspace.

Neither descent needs the sources of the sieve's arrows to be affine, only the members of the
generating family: both ideals are presheaves on the small étale site
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_map` and the restriction map of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.vanishingPresheafIdeal`), so a section lying in one over a generating
chart lies in it over anything above that chart.  This is what makes the generated sieve usable
even though being good for `g` is not inherited by a further étale chart.

